  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Both SPE reads enumerate every page; a service-level test proves a multi-page container is fully seen.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">container_not_cleared reports incomplete when a member on page 2 survives.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">/revoke's status matches closure's for the identical failure shape, using the same reason-code family.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Perturbation: returning only page 1 fails at least one test; re-swallowing the revoke failure fails at least one test.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">All 7 test projects pass; publish size reported and &lt;=60 MB.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>✅ CLOSED 2026-09-09 — **M1 only. M2 was deliberately NOT done and is now task 065's.**
See notes/task-024-spe-paging-parity.md. Four things worth carrying forward:

1. STEP 0 RE-RANKED THE FINDING. The code defect is verified (single GetAsync, no nextLink, 3 sites).
   The EXPLOITABILITY is not: the SPE docs list $skip/$top and NOT $skiptoken, and the sample response
   carries no @odata.nextLink — evidence LEANING AGAINST this endpoint paging at all. A live probe 403s
   on the CLI token (needs the BFF app identity → task 047). Fixed anyway, on a better argument than
   "it might page": ignoring @odata.nextLink is an OData PROTOCOL violation independent of current
   server behaviour, and a service may begin server-driven paging at any time without that being a
   breaking change. Severity: live false-assurance → latent protocol + real honesty defect.

2. THE DESIGN'S NAMED PRECEDENT WAS SUPERSEDED. SpeAdminGraphService.ListContainerPermissionsAsync
   (:3097) ALREADY pages this exact collection correctly (while + WithUrl(OdataNextLink)) — better than
   the PrivilegeGroupResolver PageIterator the design named, which double-counts page 1 (ISS-001). The
   repo was internally INCONSISTENT: SpeAdmin paged container permissions, ExternalAccess did not.
   Deviation from POML step 1 / design §2.2 ("use PageIterator") recorded: a page BOUND is far more
   directly expressible in a plain loop.

3. NO ENUM CHANGE, as the design predicted. NoPermissionFound already promised "read successfully …
   genuinely absent"; a single-page read made that sentence false. The rule is now the pure function
   ClassifyRevokeResult, and SpeBulkRemovalResult.IsComplete gained the EnumerationComplete conjunct —
   Failed == 0 alone was a false clean, because members nobody enumerated cannot FAIL to be removed.
   ProjectClosureEndpoint needed ZERO change.

4. Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt; is banned, but a fake Kiota IRequestAdapter is NOT the same thing — it is
   the SDK's own published seam, and GraphServiceClient is built from it. That turned design §5's
   live-gated layer 3 into 14 tests that ALWAYS RUN and actually prove the loop follows the link.
   Perturbations: page-1-only → 8 · re-swallow → 2 · bypass the reader → 7 (1 ArchTest + 6 behavioural,
   caught independently) · drop the enumeration conjunct → 2. None failed zero.

⚠️ ACCEPTANCE CRITERION 3 (/revoke status parity) IS OPEN, NOT MET — transferred to task 065 as a
binding constraint IN ITS POML, because M2 before the M8 client fix trades one silent wrong answer for
another. ISS-004 (#968) filed: task 020's org-revoke N+1 is AMPLIFIED by this paging (N reads → N×pages).

Review findings M1 and M2. Finishes the observability work task 017 started.

🔴 DESIGN COMPLETED 2026-09-09 — READ IT BEFORE STARTING:
notes/decisions/spe-paging-and-revoke-honesty-design.md

It supersedes task 020's one-line sketch ("one paged read + local email match + delete-by-
permission-id"), which is a direction, not a design. Four things in it change how this task
should be executed:

1. STEP 0 IS A VERIFICATION SPIKE, NOT CODING. The finding's load-bearing claim -- that SPE
   container permissions actually PAGE -- has never been verified. The CODE defect (single
   GetAsync, no nextLink follow) is verified; the EXPLOITABILITY is not. If permissions do not
   page, this is defensive robustness, not a live hole, and should be re-ranked. Establish it
   before writing code.

2. THE PRECEDENT TO COPY IS PrivilegeGroupResolver.cs:202 (Graph SDK v6 PageIterator) --
   NOT DriveItemOperations.cs:392, which only DETECTS OdataNextLink and builds a client
   skip-token URL; it never follows it. Copying that file would produce no paging at all.
   ⚠️ PrivilegeGroupResolver has its own double-count bug (it collects page 1 in a foreach AND
   hands the same response to CreatePageIterator) -- filed as ISS-001; do not replicate it.

3. NO ENUM CHANGE IS NEEDED. SpeContainerRevokeOutcome.NoPermissionFound already promises
   "read successfully ... genuinely absent". Single-page reads make that sentence FALSE. This
   task makes the code honest to a contract that already exists. The binding rule: incomplete
   enumeration + target not found => RemovalFailed, NEVER NoPermissionFound. Same for the
   closure guard: an unverified set is never "clean". See design section 3.

4. M2 ALONE ACHIEVES NOTHING OBSERVABLE, so the recommendation is to SPLIT. AccessGrantModal
   .postJson (AccessGrantModal.tsx:327-335) does `return (await res.json()) as T` with NO res.ok
   check, so changing 200 to 500 trades one silent wrong answer for another -- possibly a
   runtime throw on ProblemDetails cast to RevokeAccessResponse. M2 must land WITH the M8 client
   fix (task 065). Suggested split: 024a verification spike, 024b paging + honesty semantics
   (self-contained), 024c status parity gated on the client. See design section 4 and 6.

Test design is section 5 -- three layers with what each does and does NOT prove. The structural
guard (layer 2) is the one that defends the fix over time and is proven technology here: the
equivalent guard in task 025 was perturbation-checked and fires.</notes>

